ses that are very easy for the other splats to exploit - sunlight, daysleep, fire, frenzy, and being staked. At the lower levels they tend to be a low mid-tier splat. However as their blood potency increases, Kindred can become one of the more powerful splats. The reason for this is that Physical intensity scales with Blood Potency in a way that nothing else does. A Blood Potency 10 kindred is capable of incredible bursts - spending up to15 vitae in one turn to add <i>30 dice</i> to an attack, and if their Vitae tank is full they could potentially do this on five consecutive turns. This means anything that tries to go hand to hand with an elder Vampire is going to get obliterated. Furthermore, the vitae costs for most Disciplines is quite low - the majority of Disciplines cost only a single Vitae, even ones like Vigor, Resilience, and Celerity that scale with the number of dots invested in them. I put low exp Vampires in the bottom mid tier, but very high exp Vampires at the low end of the top tier.<br /> <br /> <b>Beasts </b>are solidly mid-tier. They have interesting powers that scale well with exp - Dragonfire in particular is going to cause agg to a lot of other splats and is a pretty easy way to allow the Beast to impose their Lair traits on the scene. The Lair traits of a Beast with high Lair rating are almost more dangerous than their Atavisms as it lets them wear down their opponents without risking harm to themselves. As an example, Corrosive plus Sealed Exits would mean that the beast just has to wait for their opponents to die. Throw in Darkness and the Shadowed Soul Atavism and their enemies won't be able to find them either. The Advanced and Epic versions of Merits that Beasts have access to also give them a few nice options. Their main drawback is that they don't have any way to heal damage while outside their lair. The lair adds an additional complication as they are much stronger while inside it, but it seems unlikely that their opponents would willingly follow them into it. Lastly, it seems like a Beast can escape from dangerous situations in the physical world by simply disappearing and taking control of their horror (p. 97 first paragraph). A Beast in their Lair would probably mover to top tier because of all the ways they can stack the environment against their foes while remaining completely immune themselves.<br /> <br /> To summarise:<br /> <br /> Top tier:<ul><li>Demons who've gone loud</li> <li>High exp Mages</li> <li>Recently awoken Mummies</li> <li>Beasts in their Lair</li> <li>Elder Vampires</li> <li>Werewolves (only at character creation)</li> </ul>Mid-tier:<ul><li>Prometheans</li> <li>Beasts</li> <li>Mummies (Sekhem 5 or less)</li> <li>Werewolves</li> <li>Deviants</li> <li>Most Vampires</li> </ul>Bottom-tier:<ul><li>Demons in cover</li> <li>Sin-Eaters</li> <li>Changelings</li> <li>Low exp Mages</li> <li>Hunters/Mortals</li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div>Mages can heal innately bashing and lethal with Mana.<br /> Also, beasts using soul communion don t disappear from physical world, but controls their horror instead of their bodies<br /> <br /> Also, a Promethean with a Ferrum Athanor can become quite strong, and if you consider the Transhuman potential innate ability they are only few dices below Vampires","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24346,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","user":"Neos01","date":". 01-14-2021, 05:58 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T02:39:14-08:00","id":1432177,"title":"","content":"Mages have the worst &quot;spend power points to heal&quot; ability of any splat that has one, (but note that's because when they use actual spells they have the best) and can remove Tilts and mental Conditions at will by spending mana. Without magic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T05:02:48-08:00","id":1432183,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, beasts using soul communion don t disappear from physical world, but controls their horror instead of their bodies </div> </div> </div>The text on page 97 seems to contradict this:<br /> <br /> &quot;The Horror is capable of acting independently, but Beasts can see through the Horror\u2019s eyes and direct its actions at any time, or <b>choose to vanish from the physical world entirely</b> and fully inhabit their Primordial self.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, a Promethean with a Ferrum Athanor can become quite strong </div> </div> </div>The Vitality Transmutation is definitely quite powerful, but it's not that much better than similar abilities possessed by other splats. Unconquered can give the Promethean 10 bonus strength at the high end but Vigor can give a Vampire 5 additional strength, and it isn't hard to build a Werewolf who gets more than 10 extra strength from his forms and abilities.<br /> <br /> The real thing that's holding Prometheans back in my opinion is that their powers tend to be a little on the expensive side (often costing 3 pyros) and there are very few really game-breaking abilities available to them (other than Rebuke the Shroud). I tend to look at a Werewolf's free regeneration in Gauru or a Vampire's ability to completely negate your attacks with Celerity (after you've invested resources into them) or turn into a cloud of mist and summon a swarm of rats to take care of you, or a Demon's ability to decide that they actually left four minutes ago, and ask how a splat would deal with those sorts of advantages. Prometheans have a lot of very solid powers, but I haven't seen anything so far that I don't think other splats have an answer for. The only exception is Rebuke the Shroud, but it seems like the solution to a Promethean with this ability is to just run away and wait for them to die<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and if you consider the Transhuman potential innate ability they are only few dices below Vampires </div> </div> </div>There are two reasons I don't consider this to be as powerful as Physical Intensity. One is that it is only half as powerful - gaining 1 dice per point rather than two. The other is that it caps out at 10 Pyros per turn rather than 15 Vitae per turn for Vampires. So in general a Vampire gets twice as many dice as a Promethean but at the very high end it's the difference between 10 bonus dice and 30 bonus dice. Being able to throw 10 bonus dice at a problem at high exp levels isn't really that great - plenty of splats can do that sort of thing for much less than 10 power points per turn - but I don't know of any that can match the Vampire's 30 dice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T05:31:01-08:00","id":1432184,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432183#post1432183\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The text on page 97 seems to contradict this:<br /> <br /> &quot;The Horror is capable of acting independently, but Beasts can see through the Horror\u2019s eyes and direct its actions at any time, or <b>choose to vanish from the physical world entirely</b> and fully inhabit their Primordial self.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The Vitality Transmutation is definitely quite powerful, but it's not that much better than similar abilities possessed by other splats. Unconquered can give the Promethean 10 bonus strength at the high end but Vigor can give a Vampire 5 additional strength, and it isn't hard to build a Werewolf who gets more than 10 extra strength from his forms and abilities.<br /> <br /> The real thing that's holding Prometheans back in my opinion is that their powers tend to be a little on the expensive side (often costing 3 pyros) and there are very few really game-breaking abilities available to them (other than Rebuke the Shroud). I tend to look at a Werewolf's free regeneration in Gauru or a Vampire's ability to completely negate your attacks with Celerity (after you've invested resources into them) or turn into a cloud of mist and summon a swarm of rats to take care of you, or a Demon's ability to decide that they actually left four minutes ago, and ask how a splat would deal with those sorts of advantages. Prometheans have a lot of very solid powers, but I haven't seen anything so far that I don't think other splats have an answer for. The only exception is Rebuke the Shroud, but it seems like the solution to a Promethean with this ability is to just run away and wait for them to die<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> There are two reasons I don't consider this to be as powerful as Physical Intensity. One is that it is only half as powerful - gaining 1 dice per point rather than two. The other is that it caps out at 10 Pyros per turn rather than 15 Vitae per turn for Vampires. So in general a Vampire gets twice as many dice as a Promethean but at the very high end it's the difference between 10 bonus dice and 30 bonus dice. Being able to throw 10 bonus dice at a problem at high exp levels isn't really that great - plenty of splats can do that sort of thing for much less than 10 power points per turn - but I don't know of any that can match the Vampire's 30 dice.</div> </div> </div> </div>About beasts, it seem that the quote you pasted doesn t refer to the Soul communion ability, but to the Open primordial pathways one.<br /> The latter can in fact summon your lair into the physical world in an instant and allow you to escape, but it s hard to use outside some resonant places or situations<br /> <br /> About prometheans, the max pyros turn is lower but the pool is greater, and plumbeum has a passive ability that double the duration of the boost, so in this case a promethean can reach levels slightly lower of vampires.<br /> What i forgot, is that a vampire in frenzy receives a great physical boost, and in this case vampires becomes clearly superior.<br /> <br /> Also, i agree that vampires have surely game breaking powers, some of them with low cost, but the mist form is more defensive, as it limits your interactions.<br /> For example it doesn t in fact allow you to summon animals on your help as this ability requires you to spill blood, and it s not possible in mist form.<br /> <br /> In conclusion i agree with you that prometheans cannot compare with mages and some demons abilities, but to me they are only a little inferior in combat versus vampires.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":24346,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","user":"Neos01","date":". 01-14-2021, 09:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T06:09:39-08:00","id":1432189,"title":"","content":"For vampires: Generally you'd never see those 30 bonus dice actually used because it's extremely risky to not have anything left for any defenses if you're fighting on those levels. And also because at least one of those Vitae is better spent on Vigor and whatever Devotions you might have. On the other hand, you can still reach a +30 dice bonus by spending 10 Vitae (leaving 5 open for other stuff, like Vigor/Resilience/healing/etc, or just for Juggernaut's Gait) for +20 and being in Frenzy for an additional +10.<br /> My character (whom I've played for probably 6 years or so by now in a combat heavy game) is also a Kerberos so there's an additional +10 dice if he leverages his monstrous (hehe) Lashing Out dice pool before combat starts or even reflexively in combat if he gets a hit in or otherwise interacts with someone. It is a specific conceptual Bloodline so it's hardly typical, but it's an idea of what a combat focused Elder can add on top of the potentially 40 dice (2*15 PI, 10 Frenzy) that comes from purely innate sources available to all vampires.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T06:26:37-08:00","id":1432192,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> About beasts, it seem that the quote you pasted doesn t refer to the Soul communion ability, but to the Open primordial pathways one.<br /> The latter can in fact summon your lair into the physical world in an instant and allow you to escape, but it s hard to use outside some resonant places or situations </div> </div> </div>It's a weird one, it's actually not part of the section on Soul Communion or Primordial Pathways but just above these two under &quot;Accessing the Lair&quot;. It's pretty easy to miss, but it seems like this is a power Beats have that lets them access their Lair without opening a Primordial Pathway (and potentially letting others follow them). I think the idea here is that if a Beast gets attacked in their lair and in the physical world at the same time, they can go deal with the problem in their Lair first, and then pop back into the physical world once the threat is resolved.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> About prometheans, the max pyros turn is lower but the pool is greater, and plumbeum has a passive ability that double the duration of the boost, so in this case a promethean can reach levels slightly lower of vampires. </div> </div> </div>Stoke the Furnace combined with the higher max Pyros definitely gives the Promethean an edge in the long run. However my experience is that most fights don't last more than a few turns, so being able to do tremendous amounts of damage in a brief spike tends to be the more effective strategy<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What i forgot, is that a vampire in frenzy receives a great physical boost, and in this case vampires becomes clearly superior. </div> </div> </div>The frenzy boost is nice. I try not to focus on it too much though since it can be hard to make your vampire frenzy in a way that's advantageous in a fight, and riding the wave isn't guaranteed. Still it's yet another thing elder vampires have going for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T06:58:45-08:00","id":1432198,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It is a specific conceptual Bloodline so it's hardly typical, but it's an idea of what a combat focused Elder can add on top of the potentially 40 dice (2*15 PI, 10 Frenzy) that comes from purely innate sources available to all vampires. </div> </div> </div>This is why I rated Elder Vampires as top tier. It's expensive, but once you start stacking Disciplines, Devotions, Bloodlines and so on, you can end up with some really insane dice pools - I've seen grapple masters that throw 32 dice in grapples before adding frenzy or blood boosts.<br /> <br /> Their main weakness is against enemies that aren't going to try to out-punch them. Mages will probably look at a frenzying elder Vampire and decide that the best thing for them is to be quarantined outside of time and space. Vampires also have real trouble against enemies who can fly and attack from range. A Demon buzzing around in the air shooting hellfire at the Vampire is a much trickier proposition than a raging Gauru.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T09:42:35-08:00","id":1432212,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432192#post1432192\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's a weird one, it's actually not part of the section on Soul Communion or Primordial Pathways but just above these two under &quot;Accessing the Lair&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Accessing the Lair&quot; describes two different phenomena \u2014 &quot;see through the Horror's eyes and direct its actions at any time&quot; or &quot;choose to vanish from the physical world entirely and fully inhabit their Primordial self&quot; \u2014 and the two subheaders under that section that describe abilities rather than general mechanics correspond to those abilities in order. A Beast who enters their Lair ceases to be in the physical world, but Soul Communion specifically tells you the consequences of being attacked in the material world while you're communing with the Horror, i.e. you lose your Defense.<br /> <br /> There is absolutely no reason to assume that Soul Communion allows you to outright disappear from a location at all, much less unconditionally.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T10:32:01-08:00","id":1432220,"title":"","content":"It's good to keep in mind that Vampires have access to Protean, which at it's 5th dot is frankly a fairly wild power. Does the opponent breathe? Do they not have immediate access to fire- or sunlight-based attacks, or maybe some kinda wind-summoning deal to keep them at bay? Are they trying to run and hide, but don't have an air-tight place to hunker down in? Gonna have a bad time.<br /> <br /> Importantly, it only really needs Blood Potency 3 to be fully usable, though if you're not a Gangrel you'll have to sip on a Gangrel at some point to learn it.<br /> <br /> As an ST, I basically asked by players to hold off on the 5th dot of abilities because of stuff like that. A Vampire having that at relatively low XP really trivializes a LOT of things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/274-enokh","name":"Enokh","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar274_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T10:50:53-08:00","id":1432227,"title":"","content":"Aside from Mages, Vampires also have the easiest access to &quot;save-or-die&quot; powers. Being successfully Mesmerized with Dominate ends most fights one way or another. If you are Frightened with Nightmare, you will at least hemorrhage an important resource and dragging out the fight is no longer an option (more important in some matchups than others), while the Grand Delusion can turn the fight topsy-turvy quickly. Majesty offers Idol as a winner at the high end.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T11:14:08-08:00","id":1432230,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Enokh</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432220#post1432220\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's good to keep in mind that Vampires have access to Protean, which at it's 5th dot is frankly a fairly wild power. Does the opponent breathe? Do they not have immediate access to fire- or sunlight-based attacks, or maybe some kinda wind-summoning deal to keep them at bay? Are they trying to run and hide, but don't have an air-tight place to hunker down in? Gonna have a bad time.<br /> <br /> Importantly, it only really needs Blood Potency 3 to be fully usable, though if you're not a Gangrel you'll have to sip on a Gangrel at some point to learn it.<br /> <br /> As an ST, I basically asked by players to hold off on the 5th dot of abilities because of stuff like that. A Vampire having that at relatively low XP really trivializes a LOT of things.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, but the mist form has half his normal speed, so you can outrun the vampire, until he is forced to hide at dawn.<br /> Also to steal the breath the vampire must still win a grapple and it s not automatic","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T16:55:34-08:00","id":1432263,"title":"","content":"Oh hey! A Splat Tier List Thread. Been a while since I've seen one of these.<br /> <br /> When approaching things like tier lists it helps to consider where this concept comes from, and the OG tier list: Dungeons &amp; Dragons 3.5e. You can read a lovely breakdown of said system here: <a href=\"https://1d4chan.org/wiki/Tier_System\" target=\"_blank\">https://1d4chan.org/wiki/Tier_System</a><br /> <br /> It's a bit of a read, so the short version is: The Tier list sets out to measure each class's ability to overcome challenges when played by an experienced player in a traditional D&amp;D module with a DM who is not explicitly trying to ruin their day. And to it's credit the list does a pretty good job at that. However, one must observe the considerable number of caveats under which it operates. The Tier list assumes that everyone is of the same skill level, has the same objectives, and most importantly is playing the same game.<br /> <br /> That last one is important, because the various splats in Chronicles of Darkness are NOT playing the same game. Each splat is designed to play it's own game, with its own unique themes and challenges. Demons are embroiled in a scifi spy thriller, Changelings a dark faerie tale, and Mummies a tragic battle against time. Sometimes these themes overlap nicely, for instance Demons &amp; Mages both play fairly nicely in their shared opposition to cosmic oppression. But even where they do crossover the 'balance' may be off, like in the case of Deviants &amp; Changelings. Both are on the run sinister forces hellbent on stripping them of their humanity, but Changelings have the means to laugh off the mortal threats that plague Deviants while most Deviants are woefully ill equipped to contend with the reality bending powers of the Fae. Thus we come to the ever present question of where do these splats crossover, and the answer tends to be: Combat.<br /> <br /> I have some theories on why this is. Many players are coming from a D&amp;D background, where combat is the crux of the traditional dungeon crawl. There is also the fact that combat is easy to abstract, due to its systematic nature and ease of imagining. Of course CofD has other systems too, Chases, Investigation, Social Influence, but these tend not to feel as dire in the abstract, and tend to require at least a smidgen of a scenario to bind them to. Combat is simple, exciting, and decisive. It's easy to say &quot;Ha, vampires can grapple good and move fast, so the mage can't react!&quot; and with the plethora of options present in every splat book one can generally flex a bit of their splat preference into their decision. But most importantly, it's cool. Action scenes are fun to watch and fun to imagine. It's also why combat is a bad measure of strength, because in CofD combat is supposed to be scary. CofD is a horror game in which violence is a horror element. That's why forcing yourself on others (physically, magically, etc.) is a threat to most morality stats. It's why down and dirty combat exists, and why even potent combatants in CofD can die relatively quickly.<br /> <br /> Now, combat tirade aside, we return to the question of how to tier the splats. The easiest answer is: &quot;Don't&quot;, but that's thread capping, and thread capping is lame. Arcane presents a more elegant answer, which I quote in part.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432083#post1432083\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As the conventional wisdom goes, it pays to think about the &quot;power ladder&quot; more by the way their power is part of the themes and atmosphere of the game. Design wisdom would probably group the main gamelines like this:<br /> <br /> Top Tier: Mages, Mummies, Demons<br /> Middle Tier: Vampire, Werewolf, Promethean, Sin-Eaters, Beasts<br /> Bottom Tier: Changelings, Hunters, Deviants<br /> <br /> ...</div> </div> </div> </div>But if we want to go by the OG 3.5 list read on, just remember to take it all with a grain of salt, because 3.5 and CofD are entirely different beasts. Also recall that the Tier list is meant to address a splat in the broadest possible terms, so just because a specific build of Vampire can jump a starting Mage does not mean they will be afforded a higher Tier (see combat tirade above).<br /> <br /> Tier 1: Mages.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Tier 1 is defined as &quot;capable of doing absolutely everything, often better than classes that specialize in that thing&quot;. That's Mages, with the right Arcana they can do just about everything anyone else can do, and a host of things no one else can do. They are much better at fighting conspiracies than Deviants, they can survive in the sun where Vampires would die, and people don't inherently try to murder them like Prometheans. No other Splat has a Mage's range of effective options, and range of options is the <b>only thing</b> the 3.5 tier list cares about.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Tier 2: Demons, Mummies<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Tier 2 encompasses the D&amp;D classes with access to insane, raw power that is capable of eclipsing lesser flexibility. Demons &amp; Mummies are the only splats with insane bursts of superhuman power baked into the splat. Honestly even among the two Demons stand a head and shoulders above, with options that simply erase challenges and no baked in power decay. But the 3.5 tier list cares not about power decay, peak power is what it evaluates and Mummies have downright silly power out the gate.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Tier 3: Werewolves, Vampires, Sin-Eaters, Changelings, Beasts<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Tier 3 is for classes &quot;capable of doing one thing quite well, while still being useful when that thing is inappropriate&quot;. All five of these Splats have a host of innate abilities that set them up nicely for most situations and access to unique abilities in the form of Gifts, Disciples, etc. that let them shine in a variety of areas, but lack the nukes Demons and Mummies can grab out the gate or the sheer outrageous breadth of Mages.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Tier 4: Prometheans, Deviants<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Tier 4 is &quot;Capable of doing one thing quite well, but often useless when encounters require other areas of expertise, or capable of doing many things to a reasonable degree of competence without truly shining.&quot; Deviants are the former, Prometheans the latter. It should be noted that these are also the splats for which fear of mortal threats is a key theme, so it should come as no surprise that they end up on the lower end of a scale meant to measure how easily a character can brush off challenges.</div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Tier 5: Hunters<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Tier 5 includes characters who are &quot;Capable of doing only one thing, and not necessarily all that well&quot;. It shouldn't come as any surprise that the splat designed to confront monsters more powerful than themselves would end up tiered below the monsters they are supposed to hunt, should it?</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","user":"PenDragon","date":". 01-14-2021, 08:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T20:38:41-08:00","id":1432277,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432161#post1432161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <b>Beasts </b>are solidly mid-tier...Their main drawback is that they don't have any way to heal damage while outside their lair.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Just wanted to point out that the Relentless Hunter Atavism gives Beasts a baseline healing only a little slower than werewolves, with a satiety expenditure option that can purge an entire health track of lethal or remove multiple points of agg.<br /> <br /> And factoring in Looming Presence and the optional Anakim Birthright from the Players Guide a Beast can have pretty insane amounts of health.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-14T23:10:59-08:00","id":1432284,"title":"","content":"I would like to voice that, while an interesting exercise, the analysis suffers from the glaring problem that Chronicles of Darkness is not Dungeons and Dragons, let alone the 3.5 edition.<br /> <br /> Not to say it's without value, but the games have different goals and parameters, and the purposes of the Tier List from the 3.5 era was designed with those goals and parameters in mind.<br /> <br /> And I'll also use this an excuse to voice the opinion of the uselessness of the exercise again.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-15T00:18:30-08:00","id":1432285,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432284#post1432284\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would like to voice that, while an interesting exercise, the analysis suffers from the glaring problem that Chronicles of Darkness is not Dungeons and Dragons, let alone the 3.5 edition.<br /> <br /> Not to say it's without value, but the games have different goals and parameters, and the purposes of the Tier List from the 3.5 era was designed with those goals and parameters in mind.<br /> <br /> And I'll also use this an excuse to voice the opinion of the uselessness of the exercise again.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that a simple tier list is not very useful.<br /> But as a Game master who frequently hosts crossover chronicles, sometimes i find useful to compare each splat s abilities, it helps me to better tailor the story for the players.<br /> That said, this kind of thread tends to become quickly &quot;i put him vs her in an arena&quot;, where i would like more a discussion about a broader range of abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-15T05:50:31-08:00","id":1432305,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432277#post1432277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just wanted to point out that the Relentless Hunter Atavism gives Beasts a baseline healing only a little slower than werewolves, with a satiety expenditure option that can purge an entire health track of lethal or remove multiple points of agg.</div> </div> </div> </div>Relentless Hunter is a good power. I should have clarified that I was talking about ways of healing that are innate to the splat. but I'll admit that I hadn't considered this power.<br /> <br /> Looking at it again, it seems like taking Relentless Hunter is a bit of a no-brainer for Beasts, since it's much faster and more efficient than the standard healing in the lair in terms of satiety expenditure.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432277#post1432277\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And factoring in Looming Presence and the optional Anakim Birthright from the Players Guide a Beast can have pretty insane amounts of health.</div> </div> </div> </div>I hadn't read the Player's Guide until now. Stacking that Birthright with Looming Presence would be pretty crazy. Seems like an Anakim with both abilities, Lair 10, and the Giant merit would have a maximum health pool of 36?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neos01</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432285#post1432285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I agree that a simple tier list is not very useful.<br /> But as a Game master who frequently hosts crossover chronicles, sometimes i find useful to compare each splat s abilities, it helps me to better tailor the story for the players.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find the discussion useful as well since I tend to include all the other splats as potential allies and antagonists in my games, and it's hard to think of all the ways their abilities might interact with each other.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neos01</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432285#post1432285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, this kind of thread tends to become quickly &quot;i put him vs her in an arena&quot;, where i would like more a discussion about a broader range of abilities.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think discussion tends toward that because combat is something that all splats can do and can be easily compared. I agree that it might be interesting to look at a wider range of abilities - like comparing the social powers of Werewolves and Vampires (as an example) but you'd need some kind of framework to do it in. Werewolves don't have anything that works quite like a Vampire's dominate, and Vampires don't have anything that works quite like Unity from the Gift of Inspiration, so to compare the two splats you'd need to establish what they're trying to achieve first.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-15T08:06:51-08:00","id":1432315,"title":"","content":"I would like to propose a system based on 8 ratings. First lets start with the simple ones, based on the 3 standard attribute types:<br /> <br /> <i>Physical</i><br /> <i>Mental</i><br /> <i>Social</i><br /> <br /> These cover most basic challenges. Next we will look at ones that are more subjective, but still play an important role:<br /> <br /> <i>Volatility:</i> How likely it is to experience a fluctuation in that splat's power level. A vampire chooses to lower Blood Potency, so they are low volatility. A mage chooses to over Reach for all its worth, which is more common than a vampire. But its still sporadic, so mid volatility. A mummy doesn't choose to Descend, so high volatility.<br /> <br /> <i>Variancy:</i> How big the difference is between the splat wielding its full power is from their standard. A mage with time to prepare has high variancy, since they can have imbued and enhanced items, tass, bonuses, etc. A demon going loud or a recently risen mummy embody high variancy, so does a deviant at full power. A werewolf giving into Death Rage has medium variancy, since they can wield the garou form's power for a bit and with more precision.<br /> <br /> <i>Combat:</i> How quickly the splat can neutralize their opponents (immobilize, kill, knock out, turn in their favor, etc). The value is simply how well they could be expected to emerge when caught in single combat while fully healed, with their personal resource pool full (Aether, mana, Essence, etc) and in neutral ground.<br /> <br /> <i>Information Gathering:</i> How easily can the splat figure out what is going on. All the raw might and careful use of their power won't help much if they don't understand what is going on. <br /> <br /> <i>Requisitioning:</i> How easily the splat can be expected to secure or procure the resources and contacts they require. A vampire has the blood bond, Disciplines to support this and any favors they can procure, so very high requisitioning power. Mages have their Order archives, which leads to a medium. Werewolves need to barter or hunt for each fetish, which leads to a low.<br /> <br /> Lastly, each of those is rated on a 5 dot basis, with the general meaning outlined as follows:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>(Frail):</b> The splat has notable difficulty with this aspect, probably having penalties associated with it. Promethean's social rating are a great example, since their Disquiet makes interactions more difficult.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022 <b>(Average):</b> No great focus, but no great penalties. A splat at this level is about as competent as a mortal with equivalent training. They can be more competent than a mortal, but its not their specialty. Mages fall in this category for physical, since their bodies are still quite mortal, even when they have a few healing tricks.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>(Gifted):</b> The nature of their supernatural condition supports this pillar in a minor way. Geists are at this level due to their natural ability to perceive and understand ghosts, which is specialized, but gives them more options than most mortals.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>(Skilled):</b> This splat's default supernatural powers support this pillar in a major way. Demons with their perfect ability to lie are amazing at social, vampires due to blood bond. Vampires, werewolves and prometheans are amazing at physical. Mages are masters of investigation and changelings are great at mental due to oneiromancy and pledges. <br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>(Superb):</b> The splat not only has default supernatural powers that greatly aid in this pillar, but the available suite of powers lets them specialize in them. With an optimized build, they can face with the highest echleons in the setting (short of transcendently powerful beings like Kerberoi, archmages, Rank 6+ spirits, etc) and expect to either win or put up a strong challenge.<br /> <br /> While there are a lot more factors at play, hopefully this gives GMs a baseline that is good enough for planning challenges for players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":24280,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","user":"KaiserAfini","date":". 01-15-2021, 11:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-15T09:20:24-08:00","id":1432319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432284#post1432284\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would like to voice that, while an interesting exercise, the analysis suffers from the glaring problem that Chronicles of Darkness is not Dungeons and Dragons, let alone the 3.5 edition.<br /> <br /> Not to say it's without value, but the games have different goals and parameters, and the purposes of the Tier List from the 3.5 era was designed with those goals and parameters in mind.<br /> <br /> And I'll also use this an excuse to voice the opinion of the uselessness of the exercise again.</div> </div> </div> </div>A point I tried to drill home in my post, certainly, and the reason I quoted your Importance of Power Tier list as it's the only tier list that's even slightly applicable to CofD as it's actually intended to be played.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":148,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","user":"PenDragon","date":". 01-15-2021, 12:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-15T09:43:20-08:00","id":1432321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KaiserAfini</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432315#post1432315\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would like to propose a system based on 8 ratings. First lets start with the simple ones, based on the 3 standard attribute types:<br /> <br /> <i>Physical</i><br /> <i>Mental</i><br /> <i>Social</i><br /> <br /> These cover most basic challenges. Next we will look at ones that are more subjective, but still play an important role:<br /> <br /> <i>Volatility:</i> How likely it is to experience a fluctuation in that splat's power level. A vampire chooses to lower Blood Potency, so they are low volatility. A mage chooses to over Reach for all its worth, which is more common than a vampire. But its still sporadic, so mid volatility. A mummy doesn't choose to Descend, so high volatility.<br /> <br /> <i>Variancy:</i> How big the difference is between the splat wielding its full power is from their standard. A mage with time to prepare has high variancy, since they can have imbued and enhanced items, tass, bonuses, etc. A demon going loud or a recently risen mummy embody high variancy, so does a deviant at full power. A werewolf giving into Death Rage has medium variancy, since they can wield the garou form's power for a bit and with more precision.<br /> <br /> <i>Combat:</i> How quickly the splat can neutralize their opponents (immobilize, kill, knock out, turn in their favor, etc). The value is simply how well they could be expected to emerge when caught in single combat while fully healed, with their personal resource pool full (Aether, mana, Essence, etc) and in neutral ground.<br /> <br /> <i>Information Gathering:</i> How easily can the splat figure out what is going on. All the raw might and careful use of their power won't help much if they don't understand what is going on.<br /> <br /> <i>Requisitioning:</i> How easily the splat can be expected to secure or procure the resources and contacts they require. A vampire has the blood bond, Disciplines to support this and any favors they can procure, so very high requisitioning power. Mages have their Order archives, which leads to a medium. Werewolves need to barter or hunt for each fetish, which leads to a low.<br /> <br /> Lastly, each of those is rated on a 5 dot basis, with the general meaning outlined as follows:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>(Frail):</b> The splat has notable difficulty with this aspect, probably having penalties associated with it. Promethean's social rating are a great example, since their Disquiet makes interactions more difficult.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022 <b>(Average):</b> No great focus, but no great penalties. A splat at this level is about as competent as a mortal with equivalent training. They can be more competent than a mortal, but its not their specialty. Mages fall in this category for physical, since their bodies are still quite mortal, even when they have a few healing tricks.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>(Gifted):</b> The nature of their supernatural condition supports this pillar in a minor way. Geists are at this level due to their natural ability to perceive and understand ghosts, which is specialized, but gives them more options than most mortals.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>(Skilled):</b> This splat's default supernatural powers support this pillar in a major way. Demons with their perfect ability to lie are amazing at social, vampires due to blood bond. Vampires, werewolves and prometheans are amazing at physical. Mages are masters of investigation and changelings are great at mental due to oneiromancy and pledges.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>(Superb):</b> The splat not only has default supernatural powers that greatly aid in this pillar, but the available suite of powers lets them specialize in them. With an optimized build, they can face with the highest echleons in the setting (short of transcendently powerful beings like Kerberoi, archmages, Rank 6+ spirits, etc) and expect to either win or put up a strong challenge.<br /> <br /> While there are a lot more factors at play, hopefully this gives GMs a baseline that is good enough for planning challenges for players.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like this system quite a bit!<br /> <br /> One factor I think is worth considering if we're rating the splats &quot;in general&quot; rather than in a hyper-optimised situation is to consider how easy it is for them to fill/refill their resource tank. Geists might be top tier in this regard since they can refill their Plasm as needed simply by unlocking keys. Vampires are maybe at the 4-dot level since acquiring blood is always risky but it's also very plentiful, and it's not hard for a skilled vampire to feed from multiple mortals in the space of a few hours. Demons although they're one of the stronger splats, have trouble safely keeping their Aether topped up since the only way they have of collecting Aether that doesn't involve plugging into the God-Machine is to assume their demonic form, and that risks blowing their cover, so I would probably rate them at one dot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-15T10:47:13-08:00","id":1432325,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PenDragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432319#post1432319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A point I tried to drill home in my post, certainly, and the reason I quoted your Importance of Power Tier list as it's the only tier list that's even slightly applicable to CofD as it's actually intended to be played.</div> </div> </div> </div>[Sad Contagion Chronicle noises]","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-15T15:27:03-08:00","id":1432343,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432321#post1432321\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I like this system quite a bit!<br /> <br /> One factor I think is worth considering if we're rating the splats &quot;in general&quot; rather than in a hyper-optimised situation is to consider how easy it is for them to fill/refill their resource tank. Geists might be top tier in this regard since they can refill their Plasm as needed simply by unlocking keys. Vampires are maybe at the 4-dot level since acquiring blood is always risky but it's also very plentiful, and it's not hard for a skilled vampire to feed from multiple mortals in the space of a few hours. Demons although they're one of the stronger splats, have trouble safely keeping their Aether topped up since the only way they have of collecting Aether that doesn't involve plugging into the God-Machine is to assume their demonic form, and that risks blowing their cover, so I would probably rate them at one dot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you, glad you liked it. Regarding resupply, how about something like this:<br /> <br /> <i>Scavenging:</i> This represents the average ease that the splat has to procure the resource that fuels their supernatural abilities quickly and without relying on support infrastructure. Its rating is inversely proportional to the rarity, time commitment and average risk of refilling their supplies. Vampires have high levels, since blood is easily available, yet obtaining it at high Blood Potency requires some extra care. Mages have a medium level, since mana is neither terribly rare or common. Werewolves have a low rating, since hunting spirits is neither quick or safe. Changelings have very high levels, since Glamour is plentiful.<br /> <br /> The last factor is growth ratio. But this can be simulated by simply remaking the evaluation for new, initiate, experienced and master levels of the splat. Then sum up all the ratios to get a threat level.<br /> <br /> Based on the aggregated value, you can speculate the difficulty of an encounter. The total indicates the danger of the group, the individual values indicate how difficult it will be to neutralize that target and probably priorities (who is the best orator, most prepared spy, most dangerous ghost, etc).<br /> <br /> There will be outliers like AoE capabilities, puzzle encounters and unusual items/abilities. But I believe this system can help provide a decent foundation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":24280,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","user":"KaiserAfini","date":". 01-16-2021, 01:46 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-16T08:35:22-08:00","id":1432408,"title":"","content":"I know this enters in very specific builds territory, bit I find it funny how few creatures can deal with a Space 3-user mage who casts Ban on the area of space around them. You could argue a Changeling can use Portaling to get out of the banned space, and maaaybe a Beast could use Skeleton Key on the boundaries of the area, treating them like portals, but other than that, you would need a form of teleportation to deal with it and that's hilarious.<br /> <br /> (In theory you can't resist this spell directly because it doesn't target not even actually affect you)<br /> <br /> I haven't read much of werewolf, but I don't think they can enter the Hisil at will anywhere very easily, can they? That would also maybe work.<br /> <br /> Anyway, it's just something I always think when running these hypotheticals in my mind, since I almost always play Mastigos when playing Mage (although I always start at Mind 3 rather than Space)<br /> <br /> Edit: forgot to quote one of the posts about combat specifically.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12306,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12306-andrew-kaninchen","user":"Andrew Kaninchen","date":". 01-16-2021, 11:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12306-andrew-kaninchen","name":"Andrew Kaninchen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-16T10:58:29-08:00","id":1432419,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Andrew Kaninchen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432408#post1432408\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I haven't read much of werewolf, but I don't think they can enter the Hisil at will anywhere very easily, can they? That would also maybe work.</div> </div> </div> </div>Werewolves usually need a locus to cross, however if they have particularly low harmony they can cross to the shadow at will, and if they have particularly high harmony they can cross back to the physical world at will.<br /> <br /> In all likelihood the trapped wolves would be stuck until the spell ends (or if any of their pack escaped, until they get the Mage responsible). However there are a couple of abilities the Werewolves could use to get around the Ban. If any of the trapped wolves know the rite Hidden Path, that seems like a clear case that would provoke Clash of Wills as this rite lets them run between the Flesh and the Shadow. Unchained from the Gift of Strength might also be able to provoke a Clash of Wills. This one would be open to interpretation, does Ban count as &quot;a supernatural power that would bind or hold (the werewolf)&quot;? If so, Unchained could be used to challenge it.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure what would happen if the spell were made indefinite in duration and the Werewolves didn't have any obvious ways to escape. Can Werewolves starve or does their regeneration keep them alive? If they can't starve, they would get increasingly screwed over time as they wouldn't be able to enter the shadow and would start hitting flesh breaking points from staying out of the shadow for too long (although a generous ST might rule that it doesn't count since they are trapped against their will). They can still peer into the shadow though, and so they might eventually be able to escape by making a deal with a spirit on the other side, or perhaps their totem spirit could help them to cross in some way. In any case, I wouldn't want to be the mage responsible once the pack escapes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-16T11:53:01-08:00","id":1432421,"title":"","content":"OK, people<br /> A personal request.<br /> In the context of this thread- say something nice about Uratha potential?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4950-lashet","name":"Lashet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"281"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-16T13:06:21-08:00","id":1432428,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lashet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432421#post1432421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK, people<br /> A personal request.<br /> In the context of this thread- say something nice about Uratha potential?</div> </div> </div> </div>They were designed to kill gods and are still quite capable of it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-16T14:03:05-08:00","id":1432432,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lashet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432421#post1432421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK, people<br /> A personal request.<br /> In the context of this thread- say something nice about Uratha potential?</div> </div> </div> </div>Werewolves are very, very strong at character creation. The ability to heal all bashing and lethal every turn, combined with at least 10 health (and more often 11-14 health) in Gauru makes them very, very difficult to kill. Some other splats can do aggravated damage at character creation, but you can quite easily create a Werewolf who absorbs up to 4 agg per turn, and has a fairly high defence, which makes it difficult for even these splats to take them down.<br /> <br /> The weakness Werewolves have is that they don't have very many abilities to incapacitate opponents, or to nullify their abilities, while other top tier splats like Mages and Demons have access to many of these kinds of abilities. Werewolves generally just become better hunters and fighters as they gain exp - which in fairness is what they are supposed to be. Having said all that, Werewolf gifts are often very cost effective (compared to say Vampiric disciplines) in terms of exp. They also have great combat focused merits - things like Embodiment Of The Firstborn, Favoured Form, Fortified Form, Living Weapon, Instinctive Defence, and Relentless Assault all enhance a Werewolf's combat abilities in ways which will be useful in nearly every fight, whereas the combat merits available to other splats tend to be much more situational.<br /> <br /> There are also a few ways in which Werewolves can become extraordinarily powerful at high exp levels, but they require much more storyteller cooperation. For example, a large pack (say with more than 5 Werewolves and as many wolf-blooded and humans) could potentially have a totem spirit which is rank 4 or even rank 5 - which would pose a serious threat to even high Gnosis Mages. Investing a lot in the totem also enables the pack to go one dot above their normal limits for select skills and attributes (as described on page 92) which you could stack with merits and the form bonuses to get Werewolves with some base stats close to 20 in Gauru, Urshul, or Urhan. A cooperative Storyteller, or a pack with access to the Fetish rite, could create some incredibly powerful Fetishes to supplement their power. The book only has a short list of example fetishes, but the guidelines for creating 5-dot fetishes include suggestions like &quot;[grants] 8-again <b>and </b>the rote quality on particular dice pools&quot; and &quot;allows the Uratha to outright break some rules such as using their Defense against mental attacks or to count themselves as the bane of something that doesn\u2019t usually suffer a bane.&quot; This last point in particular would get around the difficulty Werewolves usually have dealing agg to other splats. Fetishes are not something you can buy with exp, but it's not unreasonable to assume that a Pack with high Primal Urge is likely to have access to such items, which could help rebalance the scales against more powerful splats - imagine if Mages had to subtract the Werewolf's defence (which can easily be 10 or more at high exp) from all their spellcasting pools that target the Werewolf.<br /> <br /> Of course, the real in-world reason why Werewolves are always threatening is that they are a pack. Werewolves one-on-one can often be beaten, but they have lots of Gifts, merits, and Rites that affect and strengthen the whole pack. What's more, their hunting abilities are unparalleled. When they come for you there's a very good chance that they will get the drop on you, and have set up the environment and conditions to their advantage. By contrast, it's tough to get the drop of them, especially in their territory. Someone like a Mage might be able to just portal in and surprise them, but there's every chance that the pack's Cahalith foresaw the attack, and they've taken steps to prepare.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2384,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","user":"Aurumae","date":". 01-16-2021, 05:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-16T20:11:13-08:00","id":1432458,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lashet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432421#post1432421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OK, people<br /> A personal request.<br /> In the context of this thread- say something nice about Uratha potential?</div> </div> </div> </div>In terms of practical play, basically no one is more genuinely dangerous than the Uratha. Outside of white room scenarios where every opponent has a theoretical means to overcome them, Werewolves are horrifically dangerous, the splat is so loaded for hunting that even without Gifts or Merits they the barometer of combat for every whiteroom scenario. <br /> <br /> In other words, if CofD WAS a dungeon crawl, Uratha would be the splat people would complain is OP.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-16T21:22:09-08:00","id":1432460,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PenDragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432458#post1432458\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In terms of practical play, basically no one is more genuinely dangerous than the Uratha. Outside of white room scenarios where every opponent has a theoretical means to overcome them, Werewolves are horrifically dangerous, the splat is so loaded for hunting that even without Gifts or Merits they the barometer of combat for every whiteroom scenario.<br /> <br /> In other words, if CofD WAS a dungeon crawl, Uratha would be the splat people would complain is OP.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also, to draw a different comparison, because I've had Blades in the Dark on the brain for a good long bit now: Werewolves <i>rule</i> at the turf game.<br /> <br /> In addition to pack structure giving the average player werewolf more people to call on for assistance, the default assumptions of having territory (to say nothing of the rites and Facets that play off of it) and the fact that their near-native otherworld hews so closely to Earthly geography most of the time gives the Uratha a focus on cultivating the home-field advantage in a wide area that only Beast gets near to. (Vampire and Mummy have major mechanics built around it, as do most other folks with Safe Place modifiers/analogues, but there's a world of difference in scope between &quot;don't fight the monster in its own house&quot; and &quot;this monster <i>owns the next six blocks</i> in <i>two separate dimensions</i>.&quot;)<br /> <br /> Also, it's funny (because nomenclature) how the main other splats to angle toward teamwork and role-distribution to accomplish a very focused goal are Changeling and Hunter, both of whom fall far behind in terms of general institutional reach by dint of more disjointed routing \u2014 there's an assumption of being the underdogs with the other two that werewolves often need to be fighting gods to fall into, because their playing field spans wider and has a more consistent web of contacts to lean on.<br /> <br /> Demons and Deviants can both theoretically give the wolves a run for their money in the general &quot;doing a heist&quot; department, but the average werewolf is not a fugitive riven with justifiable paranoia about the actions of a nigh-omnipresent web of conspiracy to which they are of interest, which (as above) means their social safety net is a fair bit stronger even outside the confines of the pack.<br /> <br /> I guess what I'm getting at here is that werewolves also fill the dungeon-crawl-as-heist mold pretty damn well by virtue of being a speciated gang of criminal-adjacent folks with Connections thanks to natural role-division and involvement with the setting's most Earth-adjacent otherworld, and that's pretty neat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-17T08:59:05-08:00","id":1432514,"title":"","content":"Werewolves fight with guerrilla tactics. There is very little you can do if they want to find you, they have tons of methods. So long as you are on land, their forms let them adapt well to the chase.<br /> <br /> They are relentless, healing quickly. But good luck being so yourself. Some of their Gifts can rob you of a peaceful sleep. Some even send spirits to constantly harry the prey.<br /> <br /> They are very adept at undermining your support infrastructure if not outright slipping past it. When they strike its swift and precise, they also adapt beautifully to counter ambushes.<br /> <br /> They are an elite strike team, with abilities to support each other and synergize their attack. They are efficient killers, being able to have an efficient use of Essence and recharging it with their bite. They are stalking predators, waiting for the right moment to pounce.<br /> <br /> Above all, they are pack hunters. When they descend like a bolt of lightning, amids a storm of blood and claws, even the most veteran supernaturals can feel the pack breathing inches from their neck.<br /> <br /> Werewolves are very good at what they do: the hunt.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-17T11:43:23-08:00","id":1432525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432460#post1432460\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...In addition to pack structure giving the average player werewolf more people to call on for assistance, the default assumptions of having territory (to say nothing of the rites and Facets that play off of it) and the fact that their near-native otherworld hews so closely to Earthly geography most of the time gives the Uratha a focus on cultivating the home-field advantage in a wide area that only Beast gets near to. (Vampire and Mummy have major mechanics built around it, as do most other folks with Safe Place modifiers/analogues, but there's a world of difference in scope between &quot;don't fight the monster in its own house&quot; and &quot;this monster <i>owns the next six blocks</i> in <i>two separate dimensions</i>.&quot;)...</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, in terms of group dynamics few splats have as much support or as many options as Werewolves. Sin-Eaters are probably the closest in terms of scope, with access to the Underworld, and broad network of ghosts and mediums, and the Crew system (which almost certain took some inspiration from Pack mechanics). Changelings too, though their group dynamics are more about communal security and preparedness. A differences that can be tied back to themes, as Changelings are the prey of their otherworldly enemies, while Werewolves are the hunters (in broad strokes).<br /> <br /> Hunters have group dynamics as well, but their in-setting position as underdog limits them from having the deep territorial enforcement mechanics key to the Uratha. Same can be said for Prometheans, Demons and Deviants, for whom being \u2018on the run\u2019 is an essential theme. Meanwhile Beasts are intended to glom onto other splats coteries, but don\u2019t have much in the way of interaction with their societal mechanics (though Lair mechanics give them the most potent \u2018home world\u2019 mechanic short of Archmages).<br /> <br /> Vampires, Mages &amp; Mummies are, weirdly, too big. With world spanning societies and mechanics that allow them to flex their influence at a regional level. However, in terms of actual play this scope is generally a backdrop to their individual stories which tend towards the local. Though all three can have fantastic \u2018home bases\u2019 if they put their minds to it, but it\u2019s not the assumption that all Mages have an semi-clairvoyant extra-dimensional sanctum guarded by custom built spirits.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-19T19:10:48-08:00","id":1432741,"title":"","content":"I'm reading things like +30 dices from physical intensity and I'm thinking. I may be wrong, but every book has a chapter explaining dice rolls, and everyone of them says that ypur bonuses are capped at +5. So, my understanding is that you can apply +30 dices before taking in count penalties, but the final bonus will only be +5. Or I am not seeing something here?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Homebrews:</span></b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1492909-horodrigo-homebrews-2ed\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\"><b>Vampire Homebrews</b></span></a><br /> <span style=\"color:#0000CD\"><b>Mage Legacies:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1074696-2ed-legacy-experiment-infernal-ones\" target=\"_blank\">Infernal Ones</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1070254-2e-legacies-conversions-and-homebrew?p=1078103#post1078103\" target=\"_blank\">Daoine</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1536102-homebrew-legacy-the-changed\" target=\"_blank\">Changed</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7210-horodrigo","name":"Horodrigo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7210_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-19T19:33:18-08:00","id":1432743,"title":"","content":"That\u2019s only equipment and circumstance bonuses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-19T21:08:13-08:00","id":1432748,"title":"","content":"Supernatural powers can bypass this rule.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4950-lashet","name":"Lashet","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"281"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-20T00:06:05-08:00","id":1432754,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lashet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432748#post1432748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Supernatural powers can bypass this rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>Supernatural powers <i>specify</i> when they bypass this rule, and more commonly facilitate large dicepools by increasing the actual Traits that go into their composition. This is one of the advantages of Transhuman Potential over Physical Intensity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-20T01:17:07-08:00","id":1432759,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1432754#post1432754\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Supernatural powers <i>specify</i> when they bypass this rule, and more commonly facilitate large dicepools by increasing the actual Traits that go into their composition. This is one of the advantages of Transhuman Potential over Physical Intensity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, re-reading dice pools rules this is correct.<br /> Physical intensity and frenzy apply bonuses to rolls, while transhuman potential increases the attribute for the action duration.<br /> Then vampires would be more able to overcome penalties, like defence, but the net attack bonus cannot get over strenght+skill+vigor +5.<br /> Promethean with low azoth have smaller bonuses, but raising it they can reach similar damage output","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-20T01:47:23-08:00","id":1432762,"title":"","content":"In the VtR book only equipment modifiers specify whether they have caps or not. In the CofD book they clarified it for a bunch of new modifiers that weren't present in VtR. In one case I found (regarding Clued in the Investigation system) it's specified that the modifier can exceed +5. In all the others I found (equipments and vehicles) it's specified they can't exceed +5. Seems they learned to clarify it properly at some point after they wrote VtR.<br /> <br /> How my group deals with modifiers that aren't specified is that we adhere to the +/-5 standard when the modifier has no other limit listed. For example, when figuring out a penalty to Frenzy rolls that isn't listed in the sidebar on page 104 we wouldn't go beyond -5.<br /> However, Physical Intensity is limited by the Vitae spent on it (and, technically, is a bunch of +2 modifiers, and only equipment modifiers are specified to not be stackable to above +/-5), while the Frenzy bonus and Kerberos Lashing Out bonus both are based on Blood Potency which is a trait that's already capped.<br /> Capping any of these in +5 is pretty ugly because in the former case it leads to wasting fractions of Vitae, and in the latter because of the jarring change in how they scale between BP 1 to 5 and for elders above that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-27T03:56:10-08:00","id":1433596,"title":"","content":"It could be argued that Physical Intensity is an increase to an attribute that lasts one roll.<br /> Honestly I work on the assumption that if there was meant to be a cap the rules for PI would explain it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4714-live-bait","name":"Live Bait","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-27T04:01:45-08:00","id":1433597,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Live Bait</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1433596#post1433596\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It could be argued that Physical Intensity is an increase to an attribute that lasts one roll.<br /> Honestly I work on the assumption that if there was meant to be a cap the rules for PI would explain it.</div> </div> </div> </div>But Discipline like Vigor states clearly that they increase the attribute, while PI uses the word bonus.<br /> In the same book rules about bonus and penalty to dice pools states that they can t exceed +/- 5","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-27T04:06:25-08:00","id":1433598,"title":"","content":"Fair enough but oversights happen and Onyx Path doesn\u2019t always fine tune their wording as well as we might like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4714-live-bait","name":"Live Bait","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-27T05:12:59-08:00","id":1433600,"title":"","content":"There's been a few references to the +5 bonus cap, but does anyone have the actual page where this is stated?<br /> <br /> I can find three potential instances in Requiem 2nd edition. The first is on page 169 under <i>Rolling Dice</i>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Many rolls have modifiers, either from equipment, circumstance, or someone working against your character . Most modifiers are within the range of +3 to \u20133, though they can range as low as \u20135 or as high as +5. </div> </div> </div>This passage puts a limit on individual bonuses from circumstances and equipment, but it doesn't say that no dice roll can ever be modified by more than 5. Furthermore, it does not say that circumstance and equipment bonuses cannot be stacked beyond +5 or -5. On the next page under <i>Circumstance and Equipment</i> it goes into a bit more detail:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> the modifier from circumstances will be between +3 and \u20133, though in very rare cases ... it can range from +5 to \u20135. </div> </div> </div>So this isn't saying that +5 is some kind of hard cap, just that this is as high as circumstantial bonuses go, later it states:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most equipment offers a +1 to +3 die bonus. A top-of-the-line or custom item might give a +4 or +5 die bonus </div> </div> </div>Again, this isn't implying the existence of a hard +5 cap. It is saying that equipment alone cannot grant more than +5 to a dice roll. Taking these two together, there is nothing that indicates you couldn't get a net +10 from circumstances + equipment<br /> <br /> The final reference to a +5 cap I could find is on page 184 under <i>Equipment</i>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>Dice Bonuses</i><br /> Most equipment offers a bonus to dice rolls pertaining to its use. Multiple items can influence a given roll, but a roll should not receive more than a +5 bonus. </div> </div> </div>This just seems to be a restating of page 170 - equipment by itself cannot grant more than a +5 bonus to a dice roll. It says nothing about dice granted by other sources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-27T05:48:48-08:00","id":1433603,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aurumae</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1433600#post1433600\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's been a few references to the +5 bonus cap, but does anyone have the actual page where this is stated?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you found them all. I think it's the first example that's often taken as a rule rather than a recommendation. And I would definitely be considering it a rule, except I know how easily Onyx Path forgets to clarify different things so for me it makes more sense to see if the game becomes more enjoyable with or without that cap and consider the more enjoyable option likely to be the intended way to do it. Hell, an overly strict interpretation would even restrict resistances (Resistance Attributes to resist powers, as well as Defense) to be capped at -5, and that's just ridiculous.<br /> <br /> You're right that only equipment states that cummulative modifiers are capped in \u00b15. We do generally apply it to different &quot;sources&quot; of modifiers, but that's a house rule we've adopted rather than an actual rule. For example, we don't add more than +5 from Specialties (only been applicable in our long running VtR game) and wouldn't compound circumstantial modifiers (rain, slippery floor, loud winds, etc) to more than \u00b15 if it was relevant.<br /> Edit: Example on a modifier we wouldn't cap is the cummulative -1 penalty to Defense you take for each attack after the first one. It makes little sense that you suddenly stop being overwhelmed by additional attackers once you've already faced five. That's basically the same principle as not capping the cumulative bonuses from multiple uses of Physical Intensity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":11887,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","user":"Tessie","date":". 01-27-2021, 08:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-27T07:00:38-08:00","id":1433610,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tessie</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1432061-splats-power-ladder?p=1433603#post1433603\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're right that only equipment states that cummulative modifiers are capped in \u00b15. We do generally apply it to different &quot;sources&quot; of modifiers, but that's a house rule we've adopted rather than an actual rule. For example, we don't add more than +5 from Specialties (only been applicable in our long running VtR game) and wouldn't compound circumstantial modifiers (rain, slippery floor, loud winds, etc) to more than \u00b15 if it was relevant.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the way our group runs it too. It rarely comes up that circumstantial or equipment modifiers would exceed \u00b15 anyway in my experience","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2384-aurumae","name":"Aurumae","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2384_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-27T07:29:04-08:00","id":1433613,"title":"","content":"I always played it with +5 bonus as a hard cap, especially when the bonus is mundane.<br /> ---<br /> <br /> I personally find it kinda hard to make a good comparison between the splats outside of the gamistic aspect. <br /> Otherwise some splatpowers are kinda hard to jufge in their effectivness - i.e. i find the power of vampires to create ghouls, blood bonds or blood addicts highly undervalued, which is counteracted by their sun-/activity-cycle problem.<br /> <br /> Mages get dot/xp-wise the most as a generic starting character especially in comparison to a vampire. I don\u00b4t remember exactly, but it was easily a jumpstart of 30+ xp to most other splats.<br /> <br /> Werewolfs, Changeling still can\u00b4t reach mages. - but are better of than vampires.<br /> <br /> Then there is the Powerstat treshold of 5/6+ after this, it even get\u00b4s messier to gauge. Mages literally leave the board. High Wyrd changelings get mindboggling powerful with dream- &amp; weaving.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Gamelines: Requiem, Lost, Awakening, Trinity Continuum: Aeon, some V5.<br /> Likes cheesecake (quark-based)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1305-maekkel","name":"maekkel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1305_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"206"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-17T13:09:46-08:00","id":1435784,"title":"","content":"I would weigh the power of any supernatural splat against the things that they are fighting. Mages might be powerful, but they are fighting the Exarch God Kings which basically rule the Universe. Werewolves are powerful fighters, but they are fighting the Idigam which are also proto godlike spirits which can spawn legions of minions. Deviants &quot;only&quot; need to fight a human conspiracy.<br /> <br /> In addition, I would look at the weaknesses of the splats as well. A Beast needs a Hero to lay an Anathema on it, but all werewolves have the Bane (silver) and Rage Anathemas all the time. This might balance it out a bit too. As for Mages, I find that Mages usually are mighty in a narrow band, but usually are facing the mysteries of the Universe. A Mage who specializes in Death might be very flat footed when facing a hostile spirit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T12:08:31-08:00","id":1436057,"title":"","content":"I would also argue that, the great combat abilities for werewolves are because they are probably the most likely to get into combat. I imagine that their vast regeneration when in garou form is to counter the hordes of enemies that they fight as well. In other games like Deathwatch or Exalted, thematically there are scenes of Space Marines or Exalted fighting off hordes of enemies. The Uratha sometimes find themselves fighting hordes of spirits, claimed, rat hosts, etc, or large enemies surrounded by minions, and the massive regeneration is needed just to keep them in the fight.<br /> <br /> Of course, once you are outnumbered 3 or 4 to 1, or more, that is probably when the damage wraparound takes place which puts pressure on the Uratha to win the fight or Perish. Either they let the timer run out, which leaves them more vulnerable, or they go into Kuruth which is where bad things happen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-04T17:51:08-08:00","id":1438053,"title":"","content":"An overlooked aspect of Uratha is their ability to force Clash of Wills on many facets while getting dice tricks such as Rote on them. With Fetishes increasing Duration/affecting many packmates at once, you are looking at a splat that dominate that aspect of gameplay. <br /> <br /> The other thing overlooked is Uratha have one of the largest options for holding Willpower and regaining it. Between Blood, Bone, Two Touchstones, and sleep, you are looking at five ways to gain Willpower. If you add the Pack mechanics where you have number of willpower equal to number of Uratha in the pack as communal pool on top of Zeal from the Gift of Ferver (which increases your WP pool), you are looking at quite a storehouse of points for both the pack and the personal Uratha can use.<br /> <br /> Nothing that goes &quot;Wow!&quot; but these are pretty noticeable and potent in their own right.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}